EXT. TORTOISE MARKET - GIANT SHELL - DAWN

A colossal tortoise, patient as a hill, plods across a salt flat. On its domed shell, a market of striped awnings and lanterns sways to each slow step.

Five merchants cluster at the crest. MAEVE THORNWICK (50s, spice-seller, apron stained saffron). Her apprentice KIT (teens, wiry, a pouch of glass beads). Rival glassblower DOMINIC ASHFORD (40s, sooty, barrel-chested). Cloth-weaver LADY ISOLDE (60s, veil of spun silver). And the market's crier, OLD PERRIN (80s, a hand bell).

The ground trembles. The tortoise slows.

MAEVE
(to everyone)
Hold your wares! She's stopping!

PERRIN
(ringing the bell)
Stopping! Stopping!

KIT
(to Maeve)
Is she sick?

MAEVE
She's listening. Quiet now.

DOMINIC
(muttering)
Last time she stopped, we lost half the lanterns.

ISOLDE
(to Dominic)
Then keep your mouth shut, and perhaps we keep the other half.

DOMINIC
(to himself)
Old witch.

ISOLDE
I heard that.

A deep groan rolls through the shell. Crates slide. A lantern shatters.

PERRIN
(to everyone, louder)
Brace! Brace!

KIT
(to himself)
Don't fall, don't fall, don't fall.

MAEVE
Everyone, hands on the rails! Now!

The tortoise rises on its legs, lifting the whole market toward the sky.
